.TH KILLBILLCTL 1 "2026-09-09" "killbill-rs 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
killbillctl \- command-line client for the killbill-rs daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B killbillctl
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B killbillctl
is the dependable, scriptable path to
.BR killbilld (8):
everything the
.BR killbill-tui (1)
client can do, the CLI can do. It is a pure client of the control protocol over
the
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
Unix socket and never touches the config file or daemon state directly.
.PP
State-changing commands require
.B uid 0
on the connecting process (the socket is mode
.B 0660
owned
.BR root:root ).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to the daemon's control socket. Default:
.IR /run/killbilld.sock .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help. Works after a subcommand too.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print armed state, dry-run flag, power action, sensor health, the sticky
events-lost flag, whitelist and tracked-device counts, and config health
.RB ( ok ", " "CONFIG INVALID" ", " STALE ).
.TP
.B devices
List the USB devices the daemon is currently tracking. (v1 sees only devices
added since the daemon started.)
.TP
.B arm
Raise protection. Refused, loudly, if the config is invalid, the sensor is not
running, or a sensor gap was reported under
.BR "on_sensor_gap = warn" .
.TP
.B disarm
Lower protection. Logged. This is the
.I only
way to disarm \(en signals never do.
.TP
.B test
Dry-run the policy against the currently connected devices and print what would
fire. Touches nothing.
.TP
.B reload
Re-read and re-validate
.IR /etc/killbill/config.toml .
On failure the running config and armed state are kept.
.TP
.B whitelist list
Print the whitelist.
.TP
.B whitelist add \fIID\fR [\fB\-\-label\fR \fISTRING\fR] [\fB\-\-max-count\fR \fIN\fR]
Add or replace (upsert by
.IR ID )
a whitelist entry.
.I ID
is
.I vvvv:pppp
lowercase hex, as
.BR lsusb (8)
prints. Default
.B --max-count
is 1.
.TP
.B whitelist remove \fIID\fR
Remove the entry with this id.
.TP
.B config show
Print the running (validated) configuration.
.TP
.B config set dry-run \fB<on|off>\fR
.TQ
.B config set power-action \fB<poweroff|halt|none>\fR
.TQ
.B config set armed-at-boot \fB<on|off>\fR
.TQ
.B config set sensors \fINAME\fR...
.TQ
.B config set on-sensor-gap \fB<warn|kill>\fR
Change one field. The daemon re-validates the
.I whole
config and writes nothing unless it passes \(en a change that would make the
config invalid is rejected and neither written nor applied.
.TP
.B luks engage \fB<on|off>\fR
Engage or disengage LUKS header destruction on a future kill. This is the
.I runtime
opt-in, layered on top of the config-time
.B i_understand_this_is_irreversible
acknowledgment \(en neither alone is enough. Refused unless
.B [response.luks_destroy]
is configured and acknowledged. The wipe itself is a stub in v1.
.TP
.B events \fR[\fB\-\-follow\fR]
Print the daemon's event backlog (the last 200 events), then exit. With
.BR \-\-follow ,
keep streaming live events until interrupted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed (daemon unreachable, request refused, invalid argument).
The reason is printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Set up and arm, safely:
.PP
.nf
sudo killbillctl whitelist add 1050:0407 --label "YubiKey 5C"
killbillctl test
sudo killbillctl config set dry-run on
sudo killbillctl arm
killbillctl status
.fi
.PP
Watch the daemon's decisions live:
.PP
.nf
killbillctl events --follow
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR killbilld (8),
.BR killbill-tui (1),
.BR killbill.conf (5)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at
.IR https://github.com/unrealthind/killbill-rs/issues .
